---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import NavBar from '../../components/NavBar.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
---
<Layout title="Manifesto" description="The Face Forward Manifesto — Small Choices. Better Communities.">
  <NavBar />
  <main id="main">
    <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-16 lg:px-8 lg:py-24">
      <header class="no-print mb-12 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-4 border-b rule pb-8">
        <div>
          <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[.22em] text-gold">The Manifesto</p>
          <h1 class="mt-2 font-display text-4xl font-semibold sm:text-5xl">Small Choices.<br />Better Communities.</h1>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3">
          <button
            onclick="window.print()"
            class="rounded-sm bg-gold px-4 py-2 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-forest transition-brand duration-200 hover:brightness-110"
          >
            Print / Save as PDF
          </button>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="manifesto prose-manifesto">
        <p class="lead">Every day, millions of us share parking lots with complete strangers.</p>

        <p>
          We wait for one another. We slow down for pedestrians. We leave room for shopping carts. We wave another
          driver into a space. These are small acts of courtesy that make shared spaces work.
        </p>

        <p>At Face Forward, we believe the way we park is one of those acts.</p>

        <p>
          This isn&rsquo;t about rules, winning arguments, or criticizing people who make different choices. It is
          about encouraging thoughtful habits and asking whether small, everyday decisions can have a positive impact
          when multiplied across millions of drivers.
        </p>

        <p>Our position is simple:</p>

        <p class="pullquote">
          Whenever practical, we believe parking face forward is the most considerate, efficient, and intuitive way
          to use a shared parking space.
        </p>

        <p>
          Reasonable people can disagree. We welcome that discussion. Our goal is not to shame or ridicule those who
          prefer reverse parking, but to present a thoughtful case for why forward parking has served drivers well
          for generations.
        </p>

        <h2>Why Face Forward?</h2>

        <h3>1. Efficiency</h3>
        <p>
          For many everyday parking situations, driving directly into a parking space is the simpler maneuver.
          Reversing into a confined space demands precision and often requires corrections. Reversing out opens into
          the wider aisle &mdash; more room, fewer adjustments.
        </p>
        <p>
          Individually, the difference may only be a few seconds. Across millions of parking events each day, even
          small improvements in flow benefit everyone.
        </p>

        <h3>2. Courtesy</h3>
        <p>
          Parking lots are shared spaces, and thoughtful parking considers not only our own convenience but also the
          experience of those around us.
        </p>
        <p>
          When neighboring vehicles face opposite directions, driver&rsquo;s doors frequently align, making it more
          difficult for both occupants to enter or exit comfortably at the same time. Forward-oriented parking
          naturally reduces this situation in many common parking layouts and contributes to a smoother experience
          for everyone sharing the lot.
        </p>

        <h3>3. Designed for Forward Parking</h3>
        <p>
          Much of our parking infrastructure has been designed around the expectation that vehicles will enter spaces
          from the front. Angled parking, curbside parking, and many traditional parking lot layouts reflect this
          long-established convention.
        </p>
        <p>
          Forward parking remains the default experience for most drivers and continues to align naturally with the
          way many public spaces are designed and used.
        </p>

        <h3>4. Consistency</h3>
        <p>Predictability benefits everyone.</p>
        <p>
          Whether walking through a parking lot, loading groceries, or navigating busy retail centers, consistent
          behavior makes shared spaces easier to understand and anticipate. Face Forward believes that encouraging
          common parking practices contributes to a calmer, more intuitive environment for drivers and pedestrians
          alike.
        </p>

        <h2>Our Philosophy</h2>
        <p>
          Face Forward is built on a simple belief: <strong>small acts of consideration build stronger
          communities.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Parking may seem like a minor detail, but shared public spaces are shaped by millions of small decisions
          every day. We believe that thoughtful habits &mdash; however ordinary they may seem &mdash; help create
          communities that are more efficient, more courteous, and more enjoyable for everyone.
        </p>
        <p>
          We don&rsquo;t expect everyone to agree with us. We simply invite people to think about something most of
          us have never questioned. Sometimes, progress begins with the smallest decisions.
        </p>
        <p class="pullquote">Sometimes, it begins by facing forward.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</Layout>

<style>
  .prose-manifesto :global(p) {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: rgba(245, 242, 235, 0.85);
  }
  .prose-manifesto :global(.lead) {
    font-size: 1.375rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    color: var(--color-chalk);
  }
  .prose-manifesto :global(h2) {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    font-size: 1.75rem;
  }
  .prose-manifesto :global(h3) {
    margin-top: 2.25rem;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--color-gold);
  }
  .prose-manifesto :global(.pullquote) {
    margin: 2.5rem 0;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-gold);
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.375rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--color-chalk);
  }
</style>
